Notes on the Dockerfile's build steps
=====================================

This is the glibc twin of ../musl-docker (read its NOTES first; most of it
applies here too). The toolchain is x86_64 host, x86_64-glibc-linux-gnu
target, fixed install path /opt/glibc-gcc:

- binutils: built, same reasons as for musl.
- kernel headers, then glibc: glibc is built with Ubuntu's gcc and
  binutils, both are installed straight into gcc's tooldir. No stage 1 gcc.
- gcc: built once, C and C++, no sysroot.

binutils and glibc don't depend on each other, so they are separate Docker
stages that BuildKit builds in parallel (the kernel headers stage feeds
glibc). The gcc stage copies binutils and glibc in.

Sources come from sources/ (see fetch-sources.sh), not the network.

Differences in gcc's configure flags vs musl: libsanitizer stays enabled
(glibc is what it's built for; -fsanitize=address works), and ifunc,
clock_gettime etc. need no special flags.

The gcc stage deletes its source and build trees in the same RUN as make
install. Otherwise that layer is ~8 GB, which is enough to fill the
docker VM's disk.


glibc version and patches
-------------------------

patches/ is a copy of ~/src/dl-find-object/doc/glibc/ (the _dl_find_object
fixes and the dl_events_subscribe prototype). They are against glibc master
(2.44.9000, dl_events_subscribe is GLIBC_2.45), not a release, so glibc is
the git snapshot GLIBC_COMMIT. fetch-sources.sh shallow-fetches that one
commit from sourceware and turns it into a tarball with git archive.

To move to newer patches: copy them into patches/, set GLIBC_COMMIT to the
commit they are based on (e.g. `git format-patch --base=...` records it, or
the parent of the first patch in your glibc tree), and rerun
fetch-sources.sh.


The target triple
-----------------

gcc decides whether it's a cross compiler by comparing the canonical build
and target triples. x86_64-linux-gnu canonicalizes to x86_64-pc-linux-gnu,
which is exactly what config.guess says for the build machine, so gcc would
be built native and search /usr/include, /lib, /usr/lib, ... and Ubuntu's
multiarch dirs. A different vendor field (x86_64-glibc-linux-gnu) changes
nothing about the generated code, but makes it a cross compiler that only
looks in /opt/glibc-gcc (see "No sysroot" in musl-docker's NOTES).

glibc gets the same triple as --host, so its build is a cross build too.
That mostly matters for make install: in a native build it runs ldconfig
and scripts/test-installation.pl, which compiles test programs with $CC
(Ubuntu's gcc, linking against Ubuntu's libc).


No stage 1 gcc
--------------

Like musl, glibc only needs a C compiler and binutils that produce x86_64
code: it builds with -nostdlib/-nostdinc where it matters and links libgcc.a
from the compiler it's built with. Ubuntu's gcc and binutils are new enough
(glibc wants gcc >= 12.1, binutils >= 2.39) and its hardening defaults are
handled by glibc's configure (Ubuntu builds its own glibc with that gcc).
--disable-werror because master plus a gcc newer than what glibc was last
tested with tends to produce new warnings.


Directory layout
----------------

With a prefix other than /usr, glibc puts both slibdir and rtlddir in
$prefix/lib (only --prefix=/usr gets /lib64 on x86_64). So ld.so is
$TOOLDIR/lib/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2 and libc.so.6 and friends are next to it.
ld.so's built-in search path (used after LD_LIBRARY_PATH, rpath and
ld.so.cache) is that same directory.

gcc installs libstdc++ and libgcc_s into $TOOLDIR/lib/../lib64 (the x86_64
multilib OS dir, even with --disable-multilib). $TOOLDIR/lib64 is a symlink
to lib, so they end up next to libc and ld.so finds them without rpath and
without an ld.so.cache. (The musl build uses musl's .path file for lib64
instead; glibc's equivalent would be ld.so.conf plus running ldconfig after
every change to the dirs.) Our ld searches $TOOLDIR/lib64 and $TOOLDIR/lib
by default, which is now the same directory twice; harmless.

glibc's make install also puts its programs (ldd, iconv, locale, localedef,
getconf, ...) into $TOOLDIR/bin, next to binutils' unprefixed as/ld/..., and
ldconfig, zic etc. into $TOOLDIR/sbin. None of the names clash. ldd is
handy: it's a script that runs our ld.so on the binary.

libc.so is a linker script with absolute paths
($TOOLDIR/lib/libc.so.6 etc.). Without a sysroot ld uses them as is,
which is right since glibc is installed at its final location.


sys-include and gcc's limits.h
------------------------------

For a cross gcc without a sysroot, gcc's configure and build look at the
target headers in $TOOLDIR/sys-include (not include):

- configure reads features.h there to learn the glibc version, which turns
  on things like TARGET_LIBC_PROVIDES_SSP and gnu_unique_object;
- LIMITS_H_TEST decides whether gcc's own limits.h #include_next's the
  libc's one. If not, <limits.h> lacks PATH_MAX and friends;
- fixincludes processes the headers found there.

With musl none of this mattered (gcc's linux.h puts the libc headers before
gcc's own for musl, and fixincludes is skipped). For glibc it does, so the
gcc stage makes $TOOLDIR/sys-include a symlink to include before building
gcc. gcc drops the duplicate dir from the include path (it compares
inodes). The smoke test prints PATH_MAX.


Dynamic loader at /opt/glibc-gcc
--------------------------------

- gcc hardcodes the loader path (PT_INTERP): GLIBC_DYNAMIC_LINKER64 in
  gcc/config/i386/linux64.h, sed'ed to $TOOLDIR/lib/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2.
- ld.so finds libs in its own dir (see Directory layout).
- $TOOLDIR/etc/ld.so.cache doesn't exist (cross builds don't run
  ldconfig). If you add $TOOLDIR/etc/ld.so.conf, run $TOOLDIR/sbin/ldconfig
  after changes. Note glibc reads /etc/ld.so.preload (hardcoded, not under
  sysconfdir), so the host's preload file, if any, applies.


Runtime files from the host
---------------------------

glibc derives some runtime paths from the configured prefix and hardcodes
others:

- Under $TOOLDIR: ld.so.cache, gconv modules (lib/gconv), compiled locales
  (lib/locale, empty: C and C.UTF-8 are built in; use our localedef, or
  LOCPATH, for others).
- Host /etc: nsswitch.conf, resolv.conf, hosts, gai.conf, ld.so.preload
  (hardcoded). NSS modules named in the host's nsswitch.conf that we don't
  have (systemd, mdns, ...) are skipped.
- Time zones: glibc ships no tzdata, and by default would look for it in
  $TOOLDIR/share/zoneinfo and for the local zone in $TOOLDIR/etc/localtime,
  so everything would be UTC. The glibc stage's configparms points zonedir
  and localtime-file at /usr/share/zoneinfo and /etc/localtime instead.
  (The Ubuntu docker image itself has no tzdata, so in the image it's UTC
  anyway unless tzdata is installed.)
